# CompliQ AI Compliance Chatbot — Implementation Plan

Build an agentic, tool-calling compliance chatbot on top of the existing Phase 1 RAG + report-generation architecture, without breaking any existing functionality.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Dual-database complexity**: The codebase uses MySQL (primary, JPA) + PostgreSQL (pgvector). The new `UserMemory` entity and `ChatbotTrace` entity will go in MySQL (same as `ChatSession`/`ChatMessage`). Confirm this is acceptable, or should we use a separate store?

> [!WARNING]
> **Secrets in `application.properties`**: The current `application.properties` contains plain-text AWS keys, DB passwords, and Gemini API key. This plan does NOT move them to environment variables (out of scope), but the new chatbot tracing will redact them from logs. You should migrate secrets to env vars or a vault in a follow-up.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Spring Boot 4.0.6 / Spring AI 2.0.0-M8**: These are cutting-edge milestone releases. Some APIs (especially Spring AI's tool-calling / function-calling) may differ from stable docs. The plan uses the `ChatClient` builder pattern already established in `GeminiAIService`. If Spring AI 2.0.0-M8 doesn't support `@Tool` annotations natively for Gemini, we'll implement a manual tool-dispatch loop.

## Open Questions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **1. Cost tracking pricing**: What are the per-token costs you want to use for Gemini 2.5 Flash? The plan defaults to `$0.15/1M input, $0.60/1M output` (Gemini 2.5 Flash published pricing). Adjust if needed.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **2. Human approval UX**: For high-impact actions (e.g., triggering a full compliance report, deleting documents), the plan has the agent return a `REQUIRES_APPROVAL` status with a description. The frontend would show a confirmation dialog. The user then sends a second request with an `approvalToken`. Is this acceptable, or do you want WebSocket-based real-time approval?

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **3. Memory retention policy**: The plan stores up to 50 memories per user, evicting oldest when exceeded, and caps each memory at 500 characters. Memories are only created when the LLM explicitly decides something is worth remembering. Adjust thresholds?

